    "post_rendered": "Suppose that such an integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\"  width=\"10\" height=\"8\" > exists.<br>\nfor each <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/c/e/9ce5cd6d28ae77c3099fc71fede84c4d7a04436f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1\\leq k\\leq 9$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"76\" height=\"15\" >,</span> there exists an integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/e/b5e24a94b7aabff2c1ecf896e7e55e6633137aaf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p_{k}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"17\" height=\"11\" > such that:<br>\n<br>\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/f/c/5fc596da4793422525a3695bffb9e6f9f8653581.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k.10^{p_{k}}\\leq  (n+k)! &lt; (k+1).10^{p_{k}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"250\" height=\"18\" ><br>\n<br>\nBy forming the quotient between two successive inequalities, we find that for each <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/c/b/ecb2cce3c3bd66165ac20ef440a8a65b46f6420e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2\\leq k \\leq 9$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"76\" height=\"15\" >,</span><br>\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/1/3/d136dc29181502986ac11297e57b7054b4787ddd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10^{p_{k}-p_{k-1}}&lt;n+k&lt;\\frac{k+1}{k-1}10^{p_{k}-p_{k-1}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"279\" height=\"38\" > (1)<br>\nAs <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/4/b/a4b4fcd41df3bc4cded7f4ef283472d7aaaa2e2a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{k+1}{k-1}&lt;10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"87\" height=\"38\" > for each <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/c/b/ecb2cce3c3bd66165ac20ef440a8a65b46f6420e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2\\leq k \\leq 9$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"76\" height=\"15\" >,</span> we know that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/6/6f6fbad949dfa690642e21340c8db6be4906a2fa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p_{k}-p_{k-1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"73\" height=\"11\" > is a constant (i.e. does not depends on k). Assume <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/3/f/23ff22cfa6f8a866ac7d3e77a94dbb0269b98c52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p_{k}-p_{k-1}=l$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"104\" height=\"16\" >.</span><br>\nSimply use (1) with <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/5/6/0566a0e99ebec25620d813cbbedde7217e929ed4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k=9$\"  width=\"43\" height=\"12\" >,</span> and you will find:<br>\n<br>\n<span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/3/653e4b044617f84542c871a684c7ad78963de41a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$%Error. &quot;foreach&quot; is a bad command.\n1\\leq k \\leq 9, \\ \\ 10^{l}\\leq n+k&lt;1,25.10^{l}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"272\" height=\"19\" >(</span>2)<br>\n<br>\n(by the way, this proves that if such an integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\"  width=\"10\" height=\"8\" > exists, we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/c/7/9c785c3d7eace0cb5301173b404f5e136d9afd74.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$l\\geq 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"38\" height=\"15\" > and <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/8/2/a82393b486aa9ec15f95da8e7dc045b2b38d685a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n+1\\geq 100$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"93\" height=\"15\" >)</span><br>\n<br>\nNow, from<br>\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/7/d/07dc198a4036880449b59e5a95d32f58d29a1db8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(n+1)n!=(n+1)!&lt;2.10^{p_{1}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"226\" height=\"18\" ><br>\nand<br>\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/a/cea447cf96b6e9b23aee9f0d6409d2b583968bd2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(n+4)^{4}n!&gt;(n+4)!\\geq 4.10^{p_{4}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"234\" height=\"19\" ><br>\nWe get<br>\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5db5e45cd6bb31e7c54c7965995405d54f33e9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{(n+4)^{4}}{n+1}&gt;2.10^{p_{4}-p_{1}}=2.10^{3l}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -14px\" width=\"228\" height=\"40\" ><br>\nif <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/e/0/be0c375415f4022f3a42886329cfd6833c640058.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$l\\geq 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"38\" height=\"15\" >,</span> then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/a/4/da44035500385f2497980dda96243e27aaf57977.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n+1\\geq 1000$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"102\" height=\"15\" > (using (2) ) so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/6/f/c6fb9186803a44a81ec767fdc9d446d09d1e8d44.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{n+1}{n+4}\\geq \\frac{1000}{1003}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -14px\" width=\"109\" height=\"39\" ><br>\nso:<br>\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/e/00e958ea399cf1cf200556025b49066d3b0b3f66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(n+4)^{3}=\\frac{(n+4)^{4}}{n+1}.\\frac{n+1}{n+4}&gt;\\frac{1000}{1003}2.10^{3l}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -14px\" width=\"313\" height=\"40\" ><br>\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/8/3/583e8d7b07948bf5143c2d38c1deb3e2f281a25c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n+4&gt;\\left(2\\frac{1000}{1003}\\right)^{1/3}.10^{l}&gt;1,25.10^{l}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"280\" height=\"47\" > which contradicts (2).<br>\nOtherwise, <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/6/f/e6fbb515c22e2b4d5f4b9a2b164b626a4e3e2a72.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$l=2$\"  width=\"38\" height=\"12\" >,</span> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/a/34a714d87148a6862acd6f43f967c0345201a375.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{(n+4)^{4}}{n+1}&gt;2.10^{6}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -14px\" width=\"130\" height=\"40\" ><br>\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/8/2/a82393b486aa9ec15f95da8e7dc045b2b38d685a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n+1\\geq 100$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"93\" height=\"15\" > (using (2) ) so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/8/e/88e95a6c1f2bd0ba36f66b09da740c64e4ff8fad.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{n+1}{n+4}\\geq \\frac{100}{103}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -14px\" width=\"100\" height=\"39\" ><br>\nso:<br>\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/f/adf5b505836ad6e224ef1914dc483f95a6182cf6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(n+4)^{3}=\\frac{(n+4)^{4}}{n+1}.\\frac{n+1}{n+4}&gt;\\frac{100}{103}2.10^{6}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -14px\" width=\"300\" height=\"40\" ><br>\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/7/2e7d65e993b57dfa16022e7819abd324a624feb4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n+4&gt;\\left(2\\frac{100}{103}\\right)^{1/3}.10^{2}&gt;123$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"238\" height=\"47\" ><br>\nso: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/4/3/4437b294c44e9b05dd99b87c7aaedf1488ffa828.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n+9&gt;125$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"93\" height=\"14\" > which again contradicts (2).",
    "post_canonical": "Suppose that such an integer $n$ exists.\r\nfor each $1\\leq k\\leq 9$, there exists an integer $p_{k}$ such that:\r\n\r\n$k.10^{p_{k}}\\leq  (n+k)! < (k+1).10^{p_{k}}$ \r\n\r\nBy forming the quotient between two successive inequalities, we find that for each $2\\leq k \\leq 9$,\r\n$10^{p_{k}-p_{k-1}}<n+k<\\frac{k+1}{k-1}10^{p_{k}-p_{k-1}}$ (1)\r\nAs $\\frac{k+1}{k-1}<10$ for each $2\\leq k \\leq 9$, we know that $p_{k}-p_{k-1}$ is a constant (i.e. does not depends on k). Assume $p_{k}-p_{k-1}=l$.\r\nSimply use (1) with $k=9$, and you will find:\r\n\r\n$%Error. \"foreach\" is a bad command.\n1\\leq k \\leq 9, \\ \\ 10^{l}\\leq n+k<1,25.10^{l}$(2)\r\n\r\n(by the way, this proves that if such an integer $n$ exists, we have $l\\geq 2$ and $n+1\\geq 100$)\r\n\r\nNow, from \r\n$(n+1)n!=(n+1)!<2.10^{p_{1}}$\r\nand\r\n$(n+4)^{4}n!>(n+4)!\\geq 4.10^{p_{4}}$\r\nWe get\r\n$\\frac{(n+4)^{4}}{n+1}>2.10^{p_{4}-p_{1}}=2.10^{3l}$\r\nif $l\\geq 3$, then $n+1\\geq 1000$ (using (2) ) so $\\frac{n+1}{n+4}\\geq \\frac{1000}{1003}$\r\nso:\r\n$(n+4)^{3}=\\frac{(n+4)^{4}}{n+1}.\\frac{n+1}{n+4}>\\frac{1000}{1003}2.10^{3l}$\r\n$n+4>\\left(2\\frac{1000}{1003}\\right)^{1/3}.10^{l}>1,25.10^{l}$ which contradicts (2).\r\nOtherwise, $l=2$, and $\\frac{(n+4)^{4}}{n+1}>2.10^{6}$\r\n$n+1\\geq 100$ (using (2) ) so $\\frac{n+1}{n+4}\\geq \\frac{100}{103}$\r\nso:\r\n$(n+4)^{3}=\\frac{(n+4)^{4}}{n+1}.\\frac{n+1}{n+4}>\\frac{100}{103}2.10^{6}$\r\n$n+4>\\left(2\\frac{100}{103}\\right)^{1/3}.10^{2}>123$\r\nso: $n+9>125$ which again contradicts (2).",
